+From the posting itself, or from the careers site's own pay field where there is one. Only base pay counts: a range that includes bonus, commission or equity is shown as such or left out. When a posting gives a range per level (Microsoft's "IC4" and "IC5", NVIDIA's "Level 4" and "Level 5", the company's own level names), the drawer lists each level's range under **Pay by level**, the card says "· 2 levels", and the headline range runs from the lowest to the highest. When it gives a range for each place (Amazon's pay table, Apple's and Microsoft's ranges for some areas), the drawer lists each place's own range under **Pay by place** and the card says "· N places". A posting that states only a minimum shows it as "from €46K"; an hourly rate that includes the highest shift differential says so ("Incl. top shift pay"), with the stated base beside it when the posting gives one; where a posting states a yearly figure (Austria's minimum, paid in 14 instalments) that figure is used, and the drawer says "Stated as a yearly figure" and quotes the posting. An internship's pay per hour, week or month is shown as posted, also when the posting doesn't say how long the internship lasts; it is never turned into a yearly salary, and a converted figure ("≈ $6,025 a month") is converted at the same interval. The headline median (the stats line over the chart, and Insights' median and middle half) compares one country's pay, as the chart's rows do: when the roles in view are paid in more than one currency, it is the median of the main one ("base pay median (US)"), and the rest are counted, not pooled. Part-time hourly pay is also shown as a full-time year of 2,080 hours, for comparison only. Full-time pay posted by the hour is shown at its hourly rate too ("$31.39–54.90/hour"), with its yearly figure at 2,080 hours beside it, marked as converted; sorting, the pay filter and the chart use the yearly figure. The job page gives a yearly figure exactly as the posting states it ("$119,800–234,700", "€46,400"); the card rounds it ("$120K–235K"). A bonus target the posting states as a percentage is shown as posted ("+ 20% bonus target"); a stated "+ 0% bonus target" is no bonus, and "+ bonus mentioned" means the posting mentions a bonus without a figure. None of these is added to the pay.
 
 ### How are levels read?
 
-Every role gets one of seven levels: Intern, Entry, Mid, Senior, Staff+ (staff, principal and above), Manager, and Director+ (director, head of, vice president and above). Most come from the title's own words ("Senior", "Principal", "Director", "Intern"). Where a company numbers its levels, its own ladder decides: Microsoft IC1–IC2 are Entry, IC3 Mid, IC4 Senior and IC5 and up Staff+, and M1–M5 Manager (M6 and up Director+); NVIDIA Level 1 is Entry, 2 Mid, 3 to 5 Senior and 6 and up Staff+; Google's numerals I–II are Entry, III Mid, IV Senior and V and up Staff+; Amazon's I is Entry, II Mid, III and IV Senior and V and up Staff+; L-levels such as Netflix's are Entry to L3, L4 Mid, L5 Senior and L6 and up Staff+; Meta's "(Technical Leadership)" roles are Staff+. A title with no level word shows **Level not stated**, and comparisons "at Senior level" use only postings that state one. Because the ladders differ, the same numeral can mean different levels: "III" is Mid at Google but Senior at Amazon, so the Mid chip's tip gives each board's own example.
